All seven are carried DELIBERATELY as a bundled restore per owner direction. None is accidental.

Why each matters:
- 469361 alone provides the FORCE_SHOW_NAVBAR value but is insufficient on its own.
- The SystemUI half (d3c541f4988b) is what makes the toggle live: it bypasses the
  NavigationBarControllerImpl cache for the default display, invalidates mHasNavBarOrTaskbar on
  the setting change, explicitly creates/removes the bar, and is driven by a ContentObserver on
  the FORCE_SHOW_NAVBAR URI. Without it the setting flips WMS while SystemUI keeps the cached
  answer, which is GitLab #9914 (keys off, no bar).
- Shell (da0a34e8598a) keeps Shell-side insets/DisplayLayout.hasNavigationBar consistent.
- PWM (8fc7ba0eee1a) keeps capacitive-key state and FEATURE_KEY_DISABLE consistent with the bar.
- 469362/469363 are separable features (screen unpinning without navbar; hw-key FloatingRotationButton)
  carried deliberately in the bundle.

History correction (do not repeat the "AOSP 16 caching broke it" story): the SystemUI/Shell half
existed through lineage-22.1 and was dropped by the 22.2 reduction (Gerrit 422118, merged
2025-03-28), leaving the two-file form. The NavigationBarControllerImpl cache predates that
reduction (present since 21.0). So the two-file form is insufficient on any 22.2/23.1/23.2 build;
the 23.1 implication is inference from the code shape, not a tested fact. What differs on 23.2 is
the plumbing: mHasNavBar -> mHasNavBarOrTaskbar, private shouldCreateNavBarAndTaskBar(int) ->
interface canCreateNavBarOrTaskBar(int), and the flag-gated add-decorations refill now queries WMS
instead of a hard-coded put(displayId, true).

Fixup (5e7454750e84): SettingsObserver is created only for the main display while release() runs
for every DisplayPolicy; unregister it alongside the existing observers, guarded by a null check.
